SUMMARY:The Iconic Contralto: Marian Anderson Online NEW
DESCRIPTION:This is a paid Adult School class. Click here to 
 register.\n\nMarian Anderson was a celebrated American singer and a pivotal 
 civil rights figure. Renowned for her rich voice\, she broke racial 
 barriers in classical music\, famously singing at the Lincoln Memorial in 
 1939 after being denied a venue by the Daughters of the American 
 Revolution. She was the first African American to perform at the 
 Metropolitan Opera.\n\nJoin this class to prepare you for our outing\, My 
 Lord\, What a Night!\, a theatrical performance at the George Street 
 Playhouse in New Brunswick on Sunday\, May 17. An acclaimed play by Deborah 
 Brevoort dramatizing the friendship between famed singer Marian Anderson 
 and Albert Einstein\, My Lord\, What a Night! explores their response to 
 racism after Anderson was denied a hotel room in Princeton\, NJ\, and 
 Einstein hosted her\, setting the stage for her historic civil rights 
 activism.
LOCATION:Montclair Public Library
ORGANIZER;CN="Montclair Adult School":MAILTO:adultschool@montclairlibrary.org
CATEGORIES:Adult School, Music, Talk
UID:LibCal-10306-16582043
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/16582043
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260514T223000Z
DTEND:20260514T233000Z
DTSTAMP:20260514T000000Z
SUMMARY:CANCELLED - African Dance for Beginners NEW
DESCRIPTION:This is a paid Adult School class. Click here to 
 register.\n\n\n	\n		\n			\n			Join us for our all-new African Dance 
 Workshop\, an exciting opportunity to move\, connect\, and celebrate 
 culture through rhythm and community. Led by Karen Miles of Zawadi African 
 Dance and accompanied by two live drummers\, this energizing class welcomes 
 dancers of all levels.\n			\n		\n	\n\n\nThe workshop begins with a 
 10-minute guided warm-up to prepare the body with rhythmic movement and 
 grounding techniques. Participants will then learn Dansa\, a vibrant 
 traditional dance of the Malinké people from Upper Guinea.\n\nDansa is 
 traditionally performed as a joyful celebration during community 
 gatherings\, often encouraging collective labor while strengthening unity 
 and fostering a powerful spirit of togetherness. Experience the rich 
 cultural traditions\, dynamic rhythms\, and uplifting communal energy that 
 make West African dance so inspiring.\n\nNote: This class takes place at 
 DiRasa Gallery\, 4 Lackawanna Plaza in Montclair.\n
LOCATION:Montclair - Offsite\, Montclair Public Library
ORGANIZER;CN="Montclair Adult School":MAILTO:adultschool@montclairlibrary.org
CATEGORIES:Adult School, Dance
UID:LibCal-10306-16406930
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/16406930
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260514T223000Z
DTEND:20260514T234500Z
DTSTAMP:20260514T000000Z
SUMMARY:Egypt: Beyond The Pyramids NEW
DESCRIPTION:This is a paid Adult School class. Click here to 
 register.\n\nMost people picture Egypt as pyramids\, pharaohs\, and ancient 
 temples. But Egypt is also the White Desert's surreal rock formations\, 
 salt lakes shimmering in Siwa Oasis\, and valleys where whale fossils 
 remain from a long-vanished sea. It's the unexpected green of Al-Azhar Park 
 in the heart of Cairo\, the ornate grandeur of a forgotten palace on the 
 Nile\, and the Bibliotheca Alexandrina\, a modern landmark inspired by one 
 of history’s most famous libraries. Whether you’re planning a trip or 
 curious about Egypt beyond the usual sights\, come discover a side that's 
 often overlooked.\n\nIn this engaging talk\, journalist and MSU professor 
 Jenny Montasir\, who lived and worked in Egypt for four years\, offers a 
 vivid look at both ancient landmarks and contemporary life. Whether you're 
 a traveler\, history enthusiast\, or simply curious about Egyptian 
 culture\, this session brings Egypt to life through stories\, visuals\, and 
 firsthand experience.
LOCATION:Montclair Main - Conference Room\, Montclair Public Library
ORGANIZER;CN="Montclair Adult School":MAILTO:adultschool@montclairlibrary.org
CATEGORIES:Adult School, Lectures / History & Culture
UID:LibCal-10306-16497378
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/16497378
END:VEVENT
